 ## Overview
-Topologically Defined Isosurfaces is a method for extracting a closed, oriented 2-manifold from a binary volumetric image that guarantees topological correctness by enforcing digital geometry connectedness constraints [S1]. Unlike standard Marching Cubes, which may produce self-intersections or incorrect topology in ambiguous configurations, this method modifies the local configuration table based on valid foreground/background connectedness couples (e.g., 6-voxel foreground / 18-voxel background) to ensure the resulting surface separates the foreground from the background without singularities [S1]. It builds on [Topological Correctness Guarantees](1796-topological-correctness-guarantees.md) by providing a formal proof of validity for the generated surface using combinatorial manifold theory.
